==============================================================================
PART A -- HYP-8320: is sum_r a_r comparable to H, and does the PAIR beat either?
==============================================================================
  n  classes  distinct H  distinct Sa  distinct (H,Sa)  Sa refines H?  H refines Sa?
  4        4           3            4                4           True          False
  5       12           7           11               12          False          False
  6       56          19           43               51          False          False
  7      456          77          298              417          False          False
  If neither refines the other, the two are INCOMPARABLE and the PAIR is strictly
  stronger than either -- exactly the shape of THM-506's (char, perm) result.

==============================================================================
PART B -- HYP-8390b: is the size-shift about the PRIME 2, or about CROSSINGS?
==============================================================================
  My S138 framing guessed the prime-2 story explains why log(sum a) gains a
  size-dependent shift while log H does not.  Testing the alternative:

  H:  in T1 (+) T2 every Hamiltonian path runs through ALL of T1 and then ALL of T2,
      because nothing in T2 beats anything in T1.  It CROSSES THE CUT EXACTLY ONCE,
      with NO choice about where.  Hence H(T1(+)T2) = H(T1)H(T2), no interaction.
  Sa: an out-arborescence rooted in T1 lets EACH of the |T2| vertices choose its
      parent independently -- from all |T1| vertices across the cut, or from inside
      T2.  |T2| independent crossings, each with |T1| options.  THAT is where p
      enters:  Sa(T1(+)T2) = Sa(T1) * det(p I + L_in(T2)),  p = |T1|.

  Prediction if CROSSINGS is right: the shift factor should be p*prod(p+mu) over the
  NONZERO mu -- i.e. p appears once per crossing-eligible vertex, regardless of parity.
 |T1|  |T2|      Sa(T)    Sa(T1)*det(pI+L2)    p*prod(p+mu)  p even?
    2     2          6                    6          6.0000     True
    2     3         24                   24         24.0000     True
    2     3         26                   26         26.0000     True
    3     2         24                   24         12.0000    False
    3     2         36                   36         12.0000    False
    3     3        120                  120         60.0000    False
    3     3        126                  126         63.0000    False
    3     3        180                  180         60.0000    False
    3     3        189                  189         63.0000    False
    4     2        120                  120         20.0000     True
    4     2        180                  180         20.0000     True
    4     3        720                  720        120.0000     True
    4     3        744                  744        124.0000     True
    4     3       1080                 1080        120.0000     True
    4     3       1116                 1116        124.0000     True
  ordinal-sum law holds throughout: True
  Note the law holds for BOTH parities of p -- the formula never mentions 2.
  => the size-dependence is CROSSING MULTIPLICITY, not the prime split.  My S138
     framing had the causation backwards: evenness of Sa is a CONSEQUENCE (p can be
     even), not the cause.  HYP-8390b's phrasing is REFUTED as stated.

